WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.211
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.511 --> 00:00:09.269
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.569 --> 00:00:16.865
Unit fourteen: Literature Review Methods. Today you will state the purpose of research activities with 'in order to'.

4
00:00:17.165 --> 00:00:19.620
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:23.881
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:25.985
Literature review.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:31.131
Literature review means a critical survey of existing research on a topic.

8
00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:34.726
The literature review occupies a third of the dissertation.

9
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:38.236
Your turn. Say: Literature review.

10
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.461
Literature review.

11
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:44.531
Synthesis.

12
00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:49.420
Synthesis means integrating findings to reveal patterns and debates.

13
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:53.375
Synthesis distinguishes a review from an annotated list.

14
00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:56.612
Your turn. Say: Synthesis.

15
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:00.456
Synthesis.

16
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:01.916
Gap.

17
00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:06.565
Gap means an area that existing research has not adequately addressed.

18
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.334
The study fills a gap in research on rural schools.

19
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.150
Your turn. Say: Gap.

20
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:16.641
Gap.

21
00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:18.651
Chronological.

22
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.112
Chronological means arranged in the order in which events occurred.

23
00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:26.912
A chronological review traces how ideas evolved.

24
00:01:27.517 --> 00:01:30.215
Your turn. Say: Chronological.

25
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:34.404
Chronological.

26
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.221
Thematic.

27
00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:40.215
Thematic means organised around key themes or issues.

28
00:01:40.817 --> 00:01:43.641
A thematic structure highlights competing positions.

29
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.635
Your turn. Say: Thematic.

30
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.582
Thematic.

31
00:01:51.317 --> 00:01:52.178
Scope.

32
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:56.088
Scope means the boundaries of what a review covers.

33
00:01:56.683 --> 00:02:00.599
The scope was restricted to studies published since 2015.

34
00:02:01.183 --> 00:02:03.499
Your turn. Say: Scope.

35
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:07.183
Scope.

36
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:08.916
Database.

37
00:02:09.917 --> 00:02:14.099
Database means a searchable electronic collection of academic sources.

38
00:02:14.683 --> 00:02:17.705
Specialist databases index peer-reviewed journals.

39
00:02:18.317 --> 00:02:21.015
Your turn. Say: Database.

40
00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:24.798
Database.

41
00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:26.528
Relevance.

42
00:02:27.517 --> 00:02:31.060
Relevance means the degree to which a source bears on the research question.

43
00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.288
Sources were screened for relevance and quality.

44
00:02:34.883 --> 00:02:37.454
Your turn. Say: Relevance.

45
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.336
Relevance.

46
00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:45.865
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:51.981
Visal, your supervisor says your literature review reads like a shopping list. What does she mean?

48
00:02:53.083 --> 00:03:01.784
I summarised each article separately. Researchers review prior work in order to identify a gap, not just to describe it.

49
00:03:02.883 --> 00:03:04.418
How will you restructure it?

50
00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:11.884
Thematically, so as to show where studies agree and disagree. That makes the gap far more visible.

51
00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:14.878
And how did you find your sources?

52
00:03:15.983 --> 00:03:22.232
I searched three databases in order to avoid relying on whatever a search engine ranked highest.

53
00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:24.948
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.783 --> 00:03:29.014
Grammar focus: Purpose clauses (in order to).

55
00:03:29.314 --> 00:03:30.477
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.183 --> 00:03:34.518
Researchers review prior work in order to identify a gap.

57
00:03:35.417 --> 00:03:39.483
I organised the review thematically so as to show where studies disagree.

58
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:44.031
Sources were screened in order not to include unreliable data.

59
00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:53.215
'In order to' and 'so as to' are formal ways of stating purpose. For a negative purpose, say 'in order not to' or 'so as not to', never 'in order to not'.

60
00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:58.588
Be careful! Don't say: We reviewed studies in order for identify a gap.

61
00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:04.077
Say: We reviewed studies in order to identify a gap.

62
00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.423
Use 'in order to' + base verb.

63
00:04:07.233 --> 00:04:10.269
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.067 --> 00:04:12.451
Standing on Shoulders.

65
00:04:12.751 --> 00:04:17.990
No research project begins from nothing; each builds on what others have already discovered.

66
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.262
Researchers review prior work in order to identify a gap that their own study can address.

67
00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:29.124
An effective review is therefore a synthesis rather than a series of summaries.

68
00:04:30.133 --> 00:04:36.080
Some reviews are organised chronologically so as to trace the evolution of a debate, while others are thematic.

69
00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:42.956
Whichever structure is chosen, sources must be selected for relevance in order not to dilute the argument.

70
00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:48.196
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.617
Researchers review prior work in order blank identify a gap.

72
00:04:52.917 --> 00:04:55.920
A, for. B, to. C, that.

73
00:04:59.467 --> 00:05:01.060
The answer is B: to.

74
00:05:02.217 --> 00:05:04.518
Which negative purpose clause is correct?

75
00:05:04.818 --> 00:05:10.178
A, in order to not include. B, in order not to include. C, in order not including.

76
00:05:13.733 --> 00:05:16.244
The answer is B: in order not to include.

77
00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:20.950
A review that traces how a debate evolved over time is blank.

78
00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:25.240
A, thematic. B, chronological. C, relevant.

79
00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.136
The answer is B: chronological.

80
00:05:32.283 --> 00:05:36.048
What distinguishes a literature review from a list of summaries?

81
00:05:36.348 --> 00:05:39.943
A, synthesis. B, scope. C, length.

82
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.836
The answer is A: synthesis.

83
00:05:46.983 --> 00:05:51.061
According to the reading, why must sources be selected for relevance?

84
00:05:51.361 --> 00:05:57.244
A, so as not to dilute the argument. B, to make the review longer. C, to find a database.

85
00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.752
The answer is A: so as not to dilute the argument.

86
00:06:04.967 --> 00:06:08.037
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:08.883 --> 00:06:11.963
Why is identifying a 'gap' important in a literature review?

88
00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.181
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:30.481 --> 00:07:02.073
Identifying a gap is important because it justifies the research: it shows why the study is needed. Researchers examine previous work in order to avoid simply repeating what is already known. For example, many studies have examined online learning in cities, but very few have looked at rural Cambodia. Some might argue that replicating existing studies is also valuable, and that is true. Nevertheless, a clear gap gives new research its originality and purpose.

90
00:07:02.373 --> 00:07:03.885
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:04.783 --> 00:07:09.316
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and purpose clauses (in order to).

92
00:07:09.616 --> 00:07:13.616
Today's value is thoroughness. Do the whole job, and do it well.

93
00:07:13.916 --> 00:07:16.382
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:16.682 --> 00:07:19.218
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

